The user is reporting an issue where `groupby` with a `UniqueGrouper` fails when the grouping variable is a `DataArray` with `ndim > 1`.
Looking at `UniqueGrouper.group_as_index`:

```python
    @property
    def group_as_index(self) -> pd.Index:
        """Caches the group DataArray as a pandas Index."""
        if self._group_as_index is None:
            if self.group.ndim == 1:
                self._group_as_index = self.group.to_index()
            else:
                self._group_as_index = pd.Index(np.array(self.group).ravel())
        return self._group_as_index
```

When `ndim > 1`, it uses `pd.Index(np.array(self.group).ravel())`.
If `self.group` is a `DataArray` with `ndim > 1`, `np.array(self.group)` might not be what is intended, or `ravel()` might not be enough if the data is complex.

Wait, the issue description says "groupby with a UniqueGrouper fails when the grouping variable is a DataArray with ndim > 1".

Let's see how `UniqueGrouper.factorize` uses `group_as_index`.

```python
    def factorize(self, group: T_Group) -> EncodedGroups:
        self.group = group

        if is_chunked_array(group.data) and self.labels is None:
            raise ValueError(
                "When grouping by a dask array, `labels` must be passed using "
                "a UniqueGrouper object."
            )
        if self.labels is not None:
            return self._factorize_given_labels(group)

        index = self.group_as_index
        is_unique_and_monotonic = isinstance(self.group, _DummyGroup) or (
            index.is_unique
            and (index.is_monotonic_increasing or index.is_monotonic_decreasing)
        )
        is_dimension = self.group.dims == (self.group.name,)
        can_squeeze = is_dimension and is_unique_and_monotonic

        if can_squeeze:
            return self._factorize_dummy()
        else:
            return self._factorize_unique()
```

If `ndim > 1`, `is_dimension` will be `False` because `self.group.dims` will have more than one dimension. So it will call `_factorize_unique()`.

```python
    def _factorize_unique(self) -> EncodedGroups:
        # look through group to find the unique values
        sort = not isinstance(self.group_as_index, pd.MultiIndex)
        unique_values, codes_ = unique_value_groups(self.group_as_index, sort=sort)
        if array_all(codes_ == -1):
            raise ValueError(
                "Failed to group data. Are you grouping by a variable that is all NaN?"
            )
        codes = self.group.copy(data=codes_.reshape(self.group.shape), deep=False)
        unique_coord = Variable(
            dims=codes.name, data=unique_values, attrs=self.group.attrs
        )
        full_index = (
            unique_values
            if isinstance(unique_values, pd.MultiIndex)
            else pd.Index(unique_values)
        )

        return EncodedGroups(
            codes=codes,
            full_index=full_index,
            unique_coord=unique_coord,
            coords=coordinates_from_variable(unique_coord),
        )
```

Wait, `unique_coord` is created with `dims=codes.name`. But `codes` has the same shape as `self.group`, which has `ndim > 1`.
If `self.group` has `ndim > 1`, `codes.name` is just a string (the name of the variable).
`Variable(dims=codes.name, data=unique_values, ...)` expects `dims` to be a tuple of dimensions. If `codes.name` is a string, it's treated as a single dimension.
But `unique_values` is a 1D array of unique values. So `unique_coord` will have one dimension.

However, `codes` has `self.group.shape`.
If `self.group` has `ndim > 1`, `codes` also has `ndim > 1`.
The `EncodedGroups` object will have `codes` with `ndim > 1` and `unique_coord` with `ndim == 1`.

The problem might be in `_factorize_unique` when `self.group` has `ndim > 1`.
`codes = self.group.copy(data=codes_.reshape(self.group.shape), deep=False)`
`codes_.reshape(self.group.shape)` will work if `codes_` has the same number of elements as `self.group`.

